  "worked_solution": "First, let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $80$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n80=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{80-2r}{r}=\\frac{80}{r}-2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(r) & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}(\\frac{80}{r}-2) \\\\  & =40r-{r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A$, so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(r)=40-2r.\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 40-2r & =0 \\\\ r & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=20$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(20)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(20)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=20$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nTherefore, the circular sector of the largest area will have a radius of $20,$ and the area is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =40r-{r}^{2} \\\\  & =40(20)-(20)^{2} \\\\  & =400.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
